---
/**
 * Field note: "The directional-answer trap"
 * → /field-notes/directional-answer-trap/
 *
 * Its own look, and not the A/B note's either: manila spec sheet inside a
 * drafting border, IBM Plex Mono display type, clause numbering, and a title
 * block for a footer. The spine of the piece is the engineering-drawing
 * metaphor — nothing is ever perfect, which is why a real drawing states a
 * tolerance — so the design is a drawing.
 *
 * Inter is already loaded site-wide (ThemeHead.astro); only Plex Mono is
 * imported here.
 */
---

<div class="dir">
  <div class="dir-sheet">
    <div class="dir-inner">
      <!-- ============ HERO ============ -->
      <section class="dir-hero">
        <p class="dir-kicker">Field note &middot; decision quality</p>
        <h1>
          &ldquo;Give me a <span class="dir-hi">directional</span> answer.
          It doesn&rsquo;t have to be <span class="dir-strike">perfect</span>.&rdquo;
        </h1>
        <p class="dir-dek">
          A rough answer may be enough. Agree on how much error the decision
          can tolerate before choosing how much analysis to do.
        </p>
        <p class="dir-scroll" aria-hidden="true">scroll &darr;</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 1.0 ============ -->
      <section class="dir-card">
        <p class="dir-num">1.0 &nbsp;The request</p>
        <h2>The deadline matters too.</h2>
        <p>
          Someone needs to make a decision this week and wants to know whether
          the available data can help. A request for a directional answer is
          often a reasonable place to start.
        </p>
        <p>
          An answer that arrives after the budget is allocated cannot inform
          that decision. If the call is cheap to reverse, a quick estimate
          may be more useful than a detailed analysis delivered too late.
        </p>
        <p class="dir-note">
          The difficulty is agreeing on what &ldquo;rough&rdquo; allows. Without
          that conversation, the analyst and the requester may have different
          expectations of the same estimate.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 2.0 ============ -->
      <section class="dir-card">
        <p class="dir-num">2.0 &nbsp;Term one</p>
        <h2>Agree on what &ldquo;directional&rdquo; means.</h2>
        <p>
          I use directional to mean <strong>which way, not how much</strong>: up
          or down, better or worse. Check that the requester means the same
          thing.
        </p>
        <p>
          Even that narrower claim needs evidence. An estimate above zero may
          still be too uncertain to tell you the direction.
        </p>

        <figure class="dir-fig">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 340 150" role="img" aria-labelledby="dir-d1">
            <title id="dir-d1"
              >Two estimates with uncertainty intervals. The first interval
              crosses zero, so its direction is unknown. The second sits
              entirely above zero, so its direction is up.</title
            >
            <path d="M150 22 V132" class="k-line k-dash k-dim-st" />
            <text x="150" y="146" class="k-cap k-dim-fill">no effect</text>

            <text x="2" y="34" class="k-cap k-red-fill k-start">sign unknown</text>
            <path d="M60 50 H270" class="k-line k-red-st" />
            <path d="M60 42 V58" class="k-line k-red-st" />
            <path d="M270 42 V58" class="k-line k-red-st" />
            <circle cx="165" cy="50" r="5" class="k-red-fill" />

            <text x="2" y="94" class="k-cap k-teal-fill k-start">sign known: up</text>
            <path d="M178 110 H305" class="k-line k-teal-st" />
            <path d="M178 102 V118" class="k-line k-teal-st" />
            <path d="M305 102 V118" class="k-line k-teal-st" />
            <circle cx="232" cy="110" r="5" class="k-teal-fill" />
          </svg>
          <figcaption>
            The first interval includes both increases and decreases. The second
            sits entirely above zero, providing stronger evidence of an
            increase at the chosen uncertainty level.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p class="dir-note">
          For a time series, check whether an apparent trend could reflect noise
          or seasonality. A fitted line alone is not enough to establish a
          meaningful change.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 3.0 ============ -->
      <section class="dir-card">
        <p class="dir-num">3.0 &nbsp;Term two</p>
        <h2>How much error is acceptable?</h2>
        <p>
          &ldquo;It doesn&rsquo;t have to be perfect&rdquo; can be meant as
          reassurance. It still leaves you to decide whether being off by
          2% or 20% is acceptable.
        </p>
        <p>
          An engineering drawing makes this explicit with a
          <strong>tolerance</strong>: 12.0 mm, &plusmn;0.2. That tells the
          machinist what variation is acceptable before anyone cuts metal.
          An estimate needs a similar conversation about acceptable error.
        </p>

        <figure class="dir-fig">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 340 155" role="img" aria-labelledby="dir-d2">
            <title id="dir-d2"
              >Two dimension lines. The first has a narrow tolerance band around
              its nominal value. The second is labeled plus or minus question
              mark and its band covers the whole line.</title
            >
            <text x="2" y="24" class="k-cap k-teal-fill k-start">12.0 &plusmn;0.2</text>
            <rect x="148" y="38" width="44" height="24" class="k-band k-teal-band" />
            <path d="M30 50 H310" class="k-line k-ink-st" />
            <path d="M30 40 V60" class="k-line k-ink-st" />
            <path d="M310 40 V60" class="k-line k-ink-st" />
            <path d="M170 32 V68" class="k-line k-teal-st" />

            <text x="2" y="104" class="k-cap k-red-fill k-start">12.0 &plusmn;?</text>
            <rect x="30" y="118" width="280" height="24" class="k-band k-red-band" />
            <path d="M30 130 H310" class="k-line k-ink-st" />
            <path d="M30 120 V140" class="k-line k-ink-st" />
            <path d="M310 120 V140" class="k-line k-ink-st" />
          </svg>
          <figcaption>
            The first drawing states an acceptable range. The second leaves it
            unspecified, so two people could read the same request differently.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 4.0 ============ -->
      <section class="dir-card dir-card--turn">
        <p class="dir-num">4.0 &nbsp;The follow-up</p>
        <h2>Ask about the decision.</h2>
        <p class="dir-ask">
          &ldquo;What decision will this estimate help you make?&rdquo;
        </p>
        <p>
          Choosing whether to try a small campaign is different from setting
          next year&rsquo;s headcount. Once the decision is clear, ask:
        </p>
        <p class="dir-ask dir-ask--alt">
          &ldquo;How wrong can this be before you&rsquo;d call it the other
          way?&rdquo;
        </p>
        <p>
          Write down the answer so you can both refer to it. If the requester
          cannot give a tolerance yet, work through a few possible estimates
          and ask what they would do with each.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 5.0 ============ -->
      <section class="dir-card">
        <p class="dir-num">5.0 &nbsp;The ladder</p>
        <h2>What can the evidence support?</h2>
        <p>
          These are useful distinctions when explaining an answer. They are
          not a universal ranking: the evidence you need depends on the
          decision and the cost of being wrong.
        </p>

        <ol class="dir-ladder">
          <li class="rung rung--0">
            <p class="rung-h"><span class="rung-k">R0</span> An unresolved direction</p>
            <p class="rung-d">
              The interval includes both increases and decreases.
              <strong>Direction remains uncertain.</strong> The range may
              still help rule out effects large enough to matter.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="rung rung--1">
            <p class="rung-h"><span class="rung-k">R1</span> A correlation</p>
            <p class="rung-d">
              Two things move together. <strong>May help with prediction.</strong>
              Higher churn among accounts that never opened the mobile app
              could help prioritize outreach. It does not establish that
              pushing them onto the app would keep them.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="rung rung--2">
            <p class="rung-h"><span class="rung-k">R2</span> A trend that beats noise</p>
            <p class="rung-d">
              The analysis accounts for noise and seasonality.
              <strong>Supports a claim about direction.</strong> That alone
              does not explain the cause or establish whether the change
              is large enough to matter.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="rung rung--3">
            <p class="rung-h"><span class="rung-k">R3</span> A signed effect with an interval</p>
            <p class="rung-d">
              The whole interval sits on one side of the agreed threshold.
              <strong>Supports a go/no-go</strong> if the analysis answers
              the decision question and its assumptions hold.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="rung rung--4">
            <p class="rung-h"><span class="rung-k">R4</span> A magnitude with an interval</p>
            <p class="rung-d">
              How big, how sure, against a stated threshold.
              <strong>Carries sizing:</strong> how much to spend, how many to
              hire, what to promise the board.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <p class="dir-note">
          Be explicit when the analysis supports a narrower claim than the
          decision requires. A correlation useful for outreach may tell you
          very little about the return on a proposed intervention.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 6.0 ============ -->
      <section class="dir-card">
        <p class="dir-num">6.0 &nbsp;The spec</p>
        <h2>Four questions before starting.</h2>

        <ol class="dir-spec">
          <li>
            <span class="sp-q">Which way, or how much?</span>
            <span class="sp-d">Do you need to know whether the effect is positive, or whether it is large enough to justify the cost? The required precision depends on that distinction.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="sp-q">What number would change your mind?</span>
            <span class="sp-d">This gives the estimate a threshold to compare against. If no result would change the decision, clarify what the analysis is intended to inform.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="sp-q">How wrong can it be before you&rsquo;d call it differently?</span>
            <span class="sp-d">Agree on the tolerance and record it in the request.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="sp-q">If we&rsquo;re wrong, when do we find out and what does the unwind cost?</span>
            <span class="sp-d">A cheap, reversible call can justify a rougher estimate. Discuss how you would notice an error and what reversing the decision would involve.</span>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <p class="dir-note">
          If a quick estimate is enough for the decision, deliver it with its
          limitations. There is no need to make the analysis more elaborate
          than the question requires.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 7.0 ============ -->
      <section class="dir-card">
        <p class="dir-num">7.0 &nbsp;The delivery</p>
        <h2>Hand back the number with its tolerance attached.</h2>
        <p>
          Include the estimate, its range, and the threshold you agreed on.
          Explain what the remaining uncertainty means for the decision.
        </p>

        <figure class="dir-fig">
          <p class="dir-fig-h">The whole answer</p>
          <p class="dir-quote">
            &ldquo;The estimate is +6%, with a range of +1% to +11%. That
            supports an increase, but the range crosses your +2% threshold.
            We cannot yet say the gain clears your bar. A small, reversible
            pilot may be reasonable; I would want more evidence before
            committing the full budget.&rdquo;
          </p>
          <figcaption>
            The direction is clearer than the decision: the entire interval is
            positive, but part of it falls below the required gain.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p class="dir-note">
          Send the estimate and range together in writing. That gives people
          something to refer to when they share the result after the meeting.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ CLOSER ============ -->
      <section class="dir-close">
        <p class="dir-num">The job</p>
        <p class="dir-shout">Trade precision for speed, and put the trade in writing.</p>
        <p>
          Before spending another day refining an estimate, check whether the
          extra precision would change the decision. Sometimes the rough
          answer is enough; sometimes the remaining uncertainty is exactly
          what matters.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ TITLE BLOCK ============ -->
      <footer class="dir-foot">
        <dl class="dir-block">
          <div><dt>Note</dt><dd>The directional-answer trap</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Field</dt><dd>Decision quality</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Drawn by</dt><dd>Alejandro Berrizbeitia</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Tolerance</dt><dd>Stated</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <p class="dir-foot-h">Read these</p>
        <p>
          Judea Pearl and Dana Mackenzie, <em>The Book of Why</em> (2018). Why a
          correlation cannot answer a question about intervening, which is rung
          R1 above.
        </p>
        <p>
          Annie Duke, <em>How to Decide</em> (2020). Drills for naming the
          threshold before you look at the data.
        </p>
        <p>
          David Spiegelhalter, <em>The Art of Uncertainty</em> (2024). How to put
          a number on what you do not know, and how to say it to someone who is
          not a statistician.
        </p>
        <p class="dir-back">
          <a href="/field-notes/">← All field notes</a>
          <a href="/">aleberriz.com</a>
        </p>
      </footer>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=IBM+Plex+Mono:wght@400;500;600&display=swap');

  /* Manila spec sheet. Scoped, so none of this reaches another note.
     `.dir` paints the full-bleed page, `.dir-sheet` is the drawing border,
     `.dir-inner` is the reading column. */
  .dir {
    --paper: #f2ede2;
    --sheet: #f8f5ee;
    --ink: #17171a;
    --body: #40444b;
    --dim: #6a6f78;
    --teal: #0a6a63;
    --red: #b4331f;
    --rule: rgba(23, 23, 26, 0.16);

    min-height: 100vh;
    padding: 0.75rem;
    background: var(--paper);
    color: var(--body);
    font-family: Inter, ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  /* Drawing sheet: double border, the way a drafting frame is ruled. */
  .dir-sheet {
    min-height: calc(100vh - 1.5rem);
    background: var(--sheet);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 4px var(--sheet), inset 0 0 0 5px var(--rule);
  }

  .dir-inner {
    max-width: 40rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 1.5rem 3.5rem;
  }

  .dir p { margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .dir strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .dir em { font-style: italic; }
  .dir a { color: var(--teal); }

  .dir-kicker,
  .dir-num,
  .dir-scroll,
  .dir-foot-h,
  .dir-fig-h,
  .rung-k,
  .dir-block dt {
    font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.13em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--dim);
  }

  /* ---- hero ---- */
  .dir-hero { padding: 3.5rem 0 3rem; }
  .dir-hero h1 {
    margin: 1.25rem 0 1.5rem;
    font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 6.4vw, 2.5rem);
    line-height: 1.22;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    text-wrap: balance;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .dir-hi {
    color: var(--teal);
    border-bottom: 3px solid var(--teal);
  }
  .dir-strike {
    color: var(--red);
    text-decoration: line-through;
    text-decoration-thickness: 3px;
  }
  .dir-dek {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink);
    max-width: 34ch;
  }
  .dir-scroll { margin-top: 2.5rem; }

  /* ---- clauses ---- */
  .dir-card,
  .dir-close {
    padding: 2.75rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .dir-card h2 {
    margin: 0.6rem 0 1.25rem;
    font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
    font-size: clamp(1.3rem, 5.2vw, 1.75rem);
    line-height: 1.24;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .dir-card--turn h2 { color: var(--teal); }

  .dir-note {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--red);
    font-size: 0.97rem;
  }

  /* ---- the two questions ---- */
  .dir-ask {
    margin: 1.75rem 0;
    padding: 1.1rem 1.2rem;
    background: rgba(10, 106, 99, 0.07);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--teal);
    font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
    font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 4.2vw, 1.3rem);
    line-height: 1.35;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--teal);
  }
  .dir-ask span {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 0.7rem;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.13em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--dim);
  }
  .dir-ask--alt {
    background: none;
    border-left-style: dashed;
  }

  /* ---- figures ---- */
  .dir-fig { margin: 1.75rem 0; }
  .dir-fig svg { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; }
  .dir-fig figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.9rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--dim);
  }
  .dir-fig-h { margin-bottom: 0.9rem; }

  .k-line { fill: none; stroke-width: 1.6; }
  .k-ink-st { stroke: var(--ink); }
  .k-dim-st { stroke: var(--dim); }
  .k-red-st { stroke: var(--red); }
  .k-teal-st { stroke: var(--teal); }
  .k-dash { stroke-dasharray: 4 5; }
  .k-red-fill { fill: var(--red); }
  .k-teal-fill { fill: var(--teal); }
  .k-dim-fill { fill: var(--dim); }
  .k-band { stroke: none; }
  .k-teal-band { fill: rgba(10, 106, 99, 0.18); }
  .k-red-band { fill: rgba(180, 51, 31, 0.1); }
  .k-cap {
    font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  /* CSS beats the SVG presentation attribute, so set anchoring from classes. */
  .dir text.k-cap { text-anchor: middle; }
  .dir text.k-start { text-anchor: start; }

  /* ---- evidence ladder ---- */
  .dir-ladder { margin: 1.75rem 0 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  .rung {
    position: relative;
    margin-left: 0.3rem;
    padding: 0.75rem 0 0.9rem 1.5rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .rung::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: -6px;
    top: 1.15rem;
    width: 10px;
    height: 10px;
    background: #cdc7b9;
  }
  .rung:last-child { border-left-color: var(--teal); }
  .rung--3::before,
  .rung--4::before { background: var(--teal); }
  .rung--3, .rung--4 { border-left-color: var(--teal); }
  .rung--0::before { background: var(--red); }
  .rung-h {
    margin: 0 0 0.3rem;
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .rung-k {
    margin-right: 0.55rem;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
  }
  .rung--0 .rung-k { color: var(--red); }
  .rung--3 .rung-k,
  .rung--4 .rung-k { color: var(--teal); }
  .rung-d { margin: 0; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.5; }

  /* ---- the four questions ---- */
  .dir-spec { margin: 1.5rem 0 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; counter-reset: sp; }
  .dir-spec li {
    padding: 0.9rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    counter-increment: sp;
  }
  .sp-q {
    display: block;
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .sp-q::before {
    content: counter(sp, decimal-leading-zero) ' / ';
    font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--teal);
  }
  .sp-d { display: block; margin-top: 0.3rem; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.5; }

  /* ---- the model answer ---- */
  .dir-quote {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 1.2rem 1.3rem;
    background: rgba(23, 23, 26, 0.04);
    border: 1px dashed var(--rule);
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* ---- closer ---- */
  .dir-close { border-top: 3px solid var(--ink); }
  .dir-close .dir-num { color: var(--ink); }
  .dir-shout {
    margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem;
    font-family: 'IBM Plex Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 6vw, 2.2rem);
    line-height: 1.16;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* ---- title block ---- */
  .dir-foot {
    padding: 2.5rem 0 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: var(--dim);
  }
  .dir-block {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 0;
    margin: 0 0 2rem;
    /* Only the top and left come from the container; each cell draws its own
       right and bottom, so the outer edge never doubles up. */
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-width: 1px 0 0 1px;
  }
  .dir-block > div {
    padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;
    border-right: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .dir-block dt { margin: 0 0 0.2rem; }
  .dir-block dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .dir-back { margin-top: 1.75rem; display: flex; gap: 1.5rem; }

  @media (min-width: 40rem) {
    .dir { padding: 1.25rem; }
    .dir-sheet { min-height: calc(100vh - 2.5rem); }
    .dir-inner { padding: 0 2.5rem 4rem; }
    .dir-block { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); }
  }

  /* ---- present mode (?present), for projection ---- */
  :global(html.present) .dir { font-size: 1.3rem; }
  :global(html.present) .dir-inner { max-width: 52rem; }
  :global(html.present) .dir-foot { display: none; }
</style>
